    # -- thumb_encoding_rules addendum (see the section below for the rest) ------
    verified('thumb.blx_needs_thumb_bit', 'thumb_encoding_rules',
             "*** `BLX Rm` TAKES THE INSTRUCTION SET FROM BIT 0 OF Rm. *** An injected stub that does `LDR R3,=<native function> ; BLX R3` with the RAW (even) address switches the core to ARM and executes garbage -- an instant freeze. Native callers reach these functions with a Thumb `BL`, so the addresses in your notes are even. Always OR in bit 0 for a BLX target. Diagnosed 2026-08-13 after a run of freezes wrongly blamed on gates and candidate counters: a black-box recorder showed the stub entering its capture block and dying on the very next BLX. The only call that ever worked was 0x0221CD29, because a cls-record slot already carries the bit. Addresses bitten: 0x021BABE8, 0x02243FA8, 0x021D269C, 0x0219A818, 0x0219C510. ⚠️ effect_verify.assert_thumb1_only does NOT catch this -- it rejects Thumb-2, not wrong-mode branches. *** IT RECURRED 2026-09-04 ON card.jet_warrior, WITH THIS FACT ALREADY IN THE KB, AND COST AN IN-GAME TEST. *** Four constants were written straight from their (even) disassembly addresses: TURNLOG_QUERY 0x021A14E8, TURNLOG_POST 0x021D2584, the zone predicate 0x021D6E40 and the Level getter 0x021B807C. The user's report was 'I hit a freeze when jet warrior is summoned ... before I believe it worked so this is a regression', and the culprit was the FIRST of them, inside the cls1 +0C condition stub -- a slot the engine evaluates while ENUMERATING the summon trigger, so the freeze landed exactly on 'the moment of summoning'. *** THE LESSON IS THAT PROSE DID NOT PREVENT IT AND A MECHANICAL CHECK DOES. Two now exist: (a) a module-level `assert v & 1` over every constant that will be a BLX target, in jet_warrior; and (b) a whole-cave sweep -- disassemble each occupied ov5_cave slot, track `ldr rX,[pc,#imm]` -> literal, and flag any `blx rX` whose tracked literal is even. The sweep reports 14/14 occupied slots clean as of 2026-09-04. RUN IT AFTER WRITING ANY STUB: neither a passing verify() nor a capstone listing of the stub can show you this, because the literal's VALUE never appears in the disassembly -- only `ldr rX,[pc,#imm]` does.",
             "raw = h.bytes_at(0x0221CD28, 2, 'ov3')\nassert raw == bytes.fromhex('38b5'), 'Gold Sarcophagus resolver moved'\nassert 0x0221CD29 & 1, 'cls-record entry points carry the Thumb bit'\n",
             ['eclipse-wyvern-findings'], addr=0x0221CD28, oracle='ov3')
